---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'; import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Research"><PageHero title="Research" intro="We combine quantum chemistry, large-scale simulation, and machine learning to study reactivity and stability in solid-state materials." showIntro={true} />
<section class="section white approach"><div class="container"><div class="approach-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Our approach</p><p class="approach-lede">Our research is organized around two overarching questions at the intersection of chemical engineering and materials science.</p></div><div class="question-grid"><article class="research-question"><span>01</span><h2>What new reaction chemistry becomes possible in materials with exotic electronic configurations?</h2></article><article class="research-question"><span>02</span><h2>What governs the stability of a material and the series of reactions that lead to its decomposition?</h2></article></div><div class="crosscutting"><p class="eyebrow">Crosscutting effort</p><p>These questions are unified by a crosscutting effort to develop the large-scale datasets, benchmarks, and open-source software infrastructure needed to make machine-learned interatomic potentials more accurate, reliable, and broadly useful across computational materials science.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container grid-2"><div class="image-frame"><img src="/images/electronic-states.png" alt="Illustration of electronic states in a porous material" /></div><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Research area 01</p><h2>Electronic structure beyond the atom.</h2><p>We use quantum-mechanical simulations and machine learning to understand how unusual electronic configurations in solid-state materials can give rise to fundamentally new forms of chemical reactivity.</p><p>By studying electronic behavior that extends beyond conventional atom-centered chemical intuition, we aim to identify new reaction pathways and connect electronic dynamics with the atomic-scale processes that govern materials chemistry.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section dark"><div class="container grid-2"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Research area 02</p><h2>Stability and decomposition under realistic conditions.</h2><p>We develop and apply computational methods to determine what governs the stability of solid-state materials and to uncover the reaction mechanisms that drive their decomposition when they are pushed beyond their limits.</p><p>Our work combines quantum-mechanical calculations, molecular dynamics, machine-learned interatomic potentials, and free-energy methods to study metastability, finite-temperature behavior, and material transformations across otherwise inaccessible length and time scales.</p></div><div class="image-frame"><img src="/images/research-cover.png" alt="Visualization of solid-state material stability" /></div></div></section>
<section class="section white"><div class="container grid-2"><div class="image-frame"><img src="/images/computational-science.png" alt="Computational materials science illustration" /></div><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Crosscutting area</p><h2>Improving machine-learned interatomic potentials.</h2><p>We develop the large-scale datasets, benchmarks, and open-source software infrastructure needed to evaluate and improve machine-learned interatomic potentials for solid-state materials.</p><p>This effort focuses on understanding where these potentials fail, improving the quantum-mechanical data used to train them, and building scalable, reproducible workflows for their use across computational materials science.</p><a class="button" href="/software">Explore our software</a></div></div></section></BaseLayout><style>.approach{padding-top:clamp(3.5rem,6vw,6rem);padding-bottom:clamp(3.5rem,6vw,5.5rem)}.approach-intro{display:grid;grid-template-columns:9rem minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:clamp(2rem,5vw,5rem);align-items:start;max-width:1040px;padding-bottom:clamp(2.5rem,5vw,4rem)}.approach-intro .eyebrow{margin:.4rem 0 0}.approach-intro p{margin:0}.approach-lede{font:400 clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.8rem)/1.2 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.025em}.question-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,6rem);padding:clamp(2.25rem,4vw,3.5rem) 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.research-question{position:relative;padding-left:clamp(3.5rem,5vw,4.75rem)}.research-question span{position:absolute;left:0;top:.15rem;display:grid;place-items:center;width:2.65rem;height:2.65rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--orange);color:#fff;font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em}.research-question h2{font-size:clamp(1.65rem,2.5vw,2.5rem);line-height:1.14;margin:0}.crosscutting{display:grid;grid-template-columns:9rem 1fr;gap:clamp(2rem,5vw,5rem);align-items:start;margin-top:clamp(2rem,3vw,3rem);padding:clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.25rem) clamp(1.5rem,3vw,3rem);background:#f4eee5;border-left:4px solid var(--orange)}.crosscutting .eyebrow,.crosscutting p{margin:0}.crosscutting p:last-child{max-width:850px;font-size:1.08rem}.image-frame{border-radius:18px;box-shadow:0 16px 40px rgba(24,24,20,.08)}.prose .button{color:#fff}@media(max-width:850px){.approach-intro{grid-template-columns:1fr}.approach-intro .eyebrow{margin:0 0 1rem}.question-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:2rem}.crosscutting{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.75rem}}@media(max-width:700px){.research-question{padding-left:3.5rem}}</style>
